# KS-692 — Wednesday's task for Ornith, VITEST (written 2026-09-16 from the files at develop M55 `48e65c435`: `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts` read whole (378 lines), the sibling suite `services/vc-issuer/src/__tests__/ks586-status-write-authorization.test.ts` read whole for the harness idiom, `packages/shared/src/middleware/index.ts:139-151` for `requireRole`, `packages/shared/src/security/keyRevokePolicy.ts:141` for the ruled tenancy helper, and `migrations/001_initial-schema.sql:759-765` for the backing table) — **KAM RULED 2026-09-16 15:03 (card `secuura-ks692-status-revoke-interim-posture`, option a): "Narrow now, bind-creator later."** PRE-MEASURED by Wednesday in a `git clone --shared` scratch clone, run from a working directory OUTSIDE it: the new test alone against the untouched tip → `3 failed | 3 passed (6)`; the new test plus the two edits below → `6 passed (6)`; the sibling suite `ks586-status-write-authorization.test.ts` → `14 passed` before and `13 passed, 0 failed` after (it is data-driven off the constant this task shrinks — see the raise notes); the whole `vc-issuer` suite → `3 failed | 111 passed (114)` before and `113 passed (113)` after; `tsc` clean.

## What is wrong (one paragraph)

`POST /api/status/:id/revoke` and `/unrevoke` enforce an **admin-class role and no tenant ownership
check at all**, so an `ISSUER_ADMIN` in any tenant can revoke or un-revoke a credential in **another
tenant's** status list — reproduced live, with controls both ways (a foreign caller against a
non-existent list still gets 404, and a non-admin `OWNER` still gets 403, so the 200 was a genuine
authorisation pass rather than a blanket accept). The reason no check exists is structural and is
visible in the file: `status.ts:76` holds status lists in `const statusListManagers = new
Map<string, StatusListManager>()`, a process-local Map with **no tenant linkage**, and the backing
table `credential_status_lists` (`migrations/001_initial-schema.sql:759-765`) declares only `id`,
`issuer_id`, `status_list`, `updated_at`, `created_at` — **no owner column, and nothing in the
service writes to it**. So there is no target tenant to hand `decideTenantAccess`
(`packages/shared/src/security/keyRevokePolicy.ts:141`), and the interlock that sank the KS-578/643
shape applies: adding a tenant comparison without giving lists an owner would refuse *every* caller.
Kam's ruling takes the other road — **narrow the gate to the platform roles now**, which is exactly
the call KS-743 made for the tenant-less `/api/events` routes ("there is no `targetTenantId` to hand
`decideTenantAccess`, so 'platform operator' is the only authorisation that is TRUE of this
surface"). Dropping `ISSUER_ADMIN` from `STATUS_WRITE_ROLES` closes the cross-tenant hole completely
and costs tenant admins the ability to write status until the owner column lands. NOT in this task:
the migration, a repository, or anything that gives a status list an owner — that is the
`bind-creator` follow-up named in the raise notes.

## Where

- **`Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts:40`** — `export const STATUS_WRITE_ROLES
  = [... 'ISSUER_ADMIN'] as const;`, the defect line. EDIT 2 replaces it.
- **`Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts:34-36`** — the three-line comment that
  says tenant scoping is not enforced and is "tracked on KS-586". Both halves are now wrong:
  KS-586 is **Done and archived**, and its stale pointer already sent a reviewer on PR #730 back to
  a defect that no longer existed (recorded on KS-692, 2026-09-01). Without a replacement comment
  the next reader sees `ISSUER_ADMIN` missing from an admin-class list and puts it back. EDIT 1
  replaces these three lines.
- `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts:42-53` — (correct) the router-level gate
  `router.use(...)` that calls `requireRole(...STATUS_WRITE_ROLES)`. Do not touch it. It already
  applies to every non-GET verb ahead of the route table, which is what makes editing the constant
  alone sufficient — and what keeps a future POST covered by default.
- `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts:45-47` — (correct) the
  `GET`/`HEAD`/`OPTIONS` early `return next()`. Reads stay authenticated-only; status lists are
  verification data. This task changes WRITES only.
- `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts:37-39` — (correct) the three comment lines
  explaining that `SUPER_ADMIN`/`super_admin` are the legacy JWT casings. They stay exactly as they
  are and they sit BETWEEN the two edits.
- `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts:76` — (correct) `statusListManagers`. The
  Map is the root cause and it is NOT in scope. Leave it.
- `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/__tests__/ks586-status-write-authorization.test.ts` —
  (correct) **do not edit it.** It reads `STATUS_WRITE_ROLES` and iterates whatever the array holds,
  so it follows this change by itself: `14 passed` before, `13 passed` after, zero failures.

## The exact change — TWO edits in `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/routes/status.ts`

Each fence below is a single replacement hunk. **Every line inside every fence carries a leading `+`
or a leading `-`, and there is no context line anywhere in them** — the context your hunks need is
named here in prose instead:

- EDIT 1's `-` lines are 34, 35 and 36. The line directly ABOVE them is line 33, which is a bare
  `//`. The line directly BELOW them is line 37,
  `// 'SUPER_ADMIN'/'super_admin' are the legacy JWT variants the existing admin`.
- EDIT 2's `-` line is line 40. The line directly ABOVE it is line 39,
  `// (see services/auth/src/routes/users.ts:90 and issuerCerts.ts ADMIN_ROLES).`. The line directly
  BELOW it is line 41, which is BLANK.

All four `-` lines are UNIQUE in the file (verified programmatically at `48e65c435`), so the order
you emit the two hunks in does not matter. Line numbers shift after EDIT 1 — anchor on the text, not
on the number. **Copy the `-` lines byte for byte.** Note that the third `-` line of EDIT 1 contains
an em-dash (`—`), not a hyphen: `decision — tracked on KS-586.` Every `+` line is ASCII only, with
no backslash anywhere and no line continuations.

**EDIT 1 — lines 34-36.** Three lines out, twenty-one in.

```
-// Tenant scoping is NOT enforced here yet: status lists live in a
-// process-local Map with no tenant linkage, and the ownership model is the
-// KS-539/KS-547/KS-586 joint authorization decision — tracked on KS-586.
+// KS-692 (Kam 2026-09-16, `secuura-ks692-status-revoke-interim-posture: a`,
+// "Narrow now, bind-creator later"): tenant scoping still cannot be enforced
+// here - status lists live in a process-local Map and `credential_status_lists`
+// has no owner column, so there is no target tenant to compare a caller
+// against. An admin-class gate that included ISSUER_ADMIN therefore let an
+// ISSUER_ADMIN in ANY tenant revoke or un-revoke ANY tenant's credential
+// (reproduced live). Until a list carries an owner, "platform operator" is the
+// only authorisation that is TRUE of this surface - the same call KS-743 made
+// for the tenant-less security-event routes. So the gate narrows to the
+// platform roles: the hole closes now, at the cost of tenant admins not
+// writing status until the owner column lands.
+//
+// THIS IS INTERIM, NOT THE ANSWER. The ownership model is already ruled -
+// `secuura-ks1116-presentation-credential-ownership-model`, choice
+// `bind-creator` (Kam 2026-09-13): the owner is the authenticated principal
+// that created the resource, and pre-existing NULL-owner rows stay readable by
+// the tenant OWNER role alone. When `credential_status_lists` gains that owner
+// column, this gate reverts to an admin-class role PLUS a per-list ownership
+// comparison via `decideTenantAccess`, and ISSUER_ADMIN comes back. Tracked on
+// KS-692 - NOT on KS-586, which is Done and archived, and whose stale pointer
+// here sent a reviewer back to a defect that no longer existed.
```

**EDIT 2 — line 40.** One line out, one in. The ONLY difference is that `, 'ISSUER_ADMIN'` is gone;
the three platform roles, the `export const`, and the `as const;` are all unchanged.

```
-export const STATUS_WRITE_ROLES = ['SYSTEM_ADMIN', 'SUPER_ADMIN', 'super_admin', 'ISSUER_ADMIN'] as const;
+export const STATUS_WRITE_ROLES = ['SYSTEM_ADMIN', 'SUPER_ADMIN', 'super_admin'] as const;
```

Touch nothing else in the file. Do not edit `ks586-status-write-authorization.test.ts`. Do not add a
migration, a repository, or an owner column.

## The test — CREATE THE NEW FILE `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/__tests__/ks692-status-write-platform-only.test.ts`

File: `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/src/__tests__/ks692-status-write-platform-only.test.ts`

**Harness:** `npm test` inside `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer`, which runs `vitest run`
(`package.json` scripts.test). Config is `Blockchain/Dev/services/vc-issuer/vitest.config.ts`
(`globals: true`, `environment: 'node'`; coverage thresholds exist but are only enforced by the
separate `test:coverage` script, so they do not gate this). To run this file alone:
`npm test -- --run src/__tests__/ks692-status-write-platform-only.test.ts`.

The shape — the `buildApp()` express harness whose middleware fakes `jwtAuthenticate` by reading an
`x-test-role` header, the route-table walk over `statusRoutes.stack`, the `beforeAll` ephemeral-port
`listen(0)` and the `afterAll` close — is copied from the sibling
`ks586-status-write-authorization.test.ts`. Like that suite, this one never touches a database, a
container or a real JWT: it mounts the real router in a throwaway express app and probes it over
loopback.

**Reproduce the body below EXACTLY as written.** Do not invent a helper, do not rename a variable,
do not collapse the cells into a loop, and do not describe the checks in your own words. Each of the
five graded cells increments `CELLS_RUN` on its FIRST line and then asserts; vitest's own reporter
is the pass/fail count. Every declaration the cells read (`EXPECTED_CELLS`, `CELLS_RUN`,
`PLATFORM_ROLES`, `TENANT_SCOPED_ROLES`, `server`, `baseUrl`, and the three functions) is declared
ABOVE the first cell, so no cell can fail on an unbound name. The file contains no backslash
anywhere; keep it that way.

```ts
/**
 * KS-692 — /api/status WRITES are PLATFORM-ONLY (interim posture).
 *
 * Kam ruled `secuura-ks692-status-revoke-interim-posture: a` (2026-09-16),
 * "narrow now, bind-creator later". A status list has no owning tenant to
 * compare against, so platform-operator is the only authorisation TRUE of this
 * surface today; ISSUER_ADMIN is off the write gate until a list has an owner.
 */
import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, afterAll } from 'vitest';
import express, { NextFunction, Request, Response } from 'express';
import type { Server } from 'http';
import { statusRoutes, STATUS_WRITE_ROLES } from '../routes/status';
import { errorHandler } from '../middleware/errorHandler';

const EXPECTED_CELLS = 5;
let CELLS_RUN = 0;
const PLATFORM_ROLES = ['SYSTEM_ADMIN', 'SUPER_ADMIN', 'super_admin'];
const TENANT_SCOPED_ROLES = ['ISSUER_ADMIN', 'ORG_ADMIN', 'ADMIN', 'OWNER'];
let server: Server;
let baseUrl = '';

function buildApp(): express.Express {
  const app = express();
  app.use(express.json());
  app.use((req: Request, _res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
    const role = req.header('x-test-role');
    if (role) {
      (req as Request & { user: unknown }).user = { userId: 'u-1', role, organizationId: 'org-1' };
    }
    next();
  });
  app.use('/api/status', statusRoutes);
  app.use(errorHandler);
  return app;
}

function writeRoutes(): string[] {
  const stack = (statusRoutes as unknown as {
    stack: Array<{ route?: { path: string; methods: Record<string, boolean> } }>;
  }).stack;
  return stack
    .filter((l) => l.route)
    .filter((l) => Object.keys(l.route!.methods).some((m) => m !== 'get' && m !== 'head'))
    .map((l) => l.route!.path);
}

async function probe(role: string | undefined): Promise<number> {
  const headers: Record<string, string> = { 'content-type': 'application/json' };
  if (role) headers['x-test-role'] = role;
  const res = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/api/status/default/revoke`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers,
    body: '{}',
  });
  return res.status;
}

beforeAll(async () => {
  const app = buildApp();
  await new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    server = app.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', () => resolve());
  });
  const address = server.address();
  if (address === null || typeof address === 'string') throw new Error('no ephemeral port');
  baseUrl = `http://127.0.0.1:${address.port}`;
});

afterAll(async () => {
  await new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.close(() => resolve()));
});

describe('KS-692 — status writes are platform-only (interim)', () => {
  it('RED 1: ISSUER_ADMIN is not on the status write gate', () => {
    CELLS_RUN += 1;
    expect([...STATUS_WRITE_ROLES]).not.toContain('ISSUER_ADMIN');
  });
  it('RED 2: every role on the write gate is a platform role', () => {
    CELLS_RUN += 1;
    const tenantScoped = [...STATUS_WRITE_ROLES].filter((r) => !PLATFORM_ROLES.includes(r));
    expect(tenantScoped).toEqual([]);
  });
  it('RED 3: every tenant-scoped admin is refused 403 on POST revoke', async () => {
    CELLS_RUN += 1;
    const seen: number[] = [];
    for (const role of TENANT_SCOPED_ROLES) seen.push(await probe(role));
    expect(seen).toEqual(TENANT_SCOPED_ROLES.map(() => 403));
  });
  it('GREEN CONTROL 4: every platform role still passes the gate', async () => {
    CELLS_RUN += 1;
    const blocked: string[] = [];
    for (const role of PLATFORM_ROLES) {
      const status = await probe(role);
      if (status === 401 || status === 403) blocked.push(`${role}:${status}`);
    }
    expect(blocked).toEqual([]);
  });
  it('GREEN CONTROL 5: the write routes stay mounted and still 401 anonymous', async () => {
    CELLS_RUN += 1;
    expect(writeRoutes().length).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(4);
    expect(await probe(undefined)).toBe(401);
  });
  it('COMPLETENESS: every graded cell above actually ran', () => {
    expect(CELLS_RUN).toBe(EXPECTED_CELLS);
  });
});
```

Cells RED 1, RED 2 and RED 3 all FAIL at the untouched tip, **by assertion**, never by a crash:
RED 1 reports `expected [ 'SYSTEM_ADMIN', 'SUPER_ADMIN', 'super_admin', 'ISSUER_ADMIN' ] not to
contain 'ISSUER_ADMIN'`; RED 2 reports `expected [ 'ISSUER_ADMIN' ] to deeply equal []`; RED 3
reports `expected [ 400, 403, 403, 403 ] to deeply equal [ 403, 403, 403, 403 ]` — the `400` is
`ISSUER_ADMIN` getting **through** the gate and being stopped only by body validation in the
handler, which is the defect stated as a number.

**The two 🟢 CONTROLS, and why the obvious one was not available.** The usual control for a security
fix is "the legitimate caller still succeeds" — but here the caller being removed, `ISSUER_ADMIN`,
IS the one in question, so that control would have to assert the thing the ticket is deleting. It
was unavailable by construction. The two chosen instead are things that genuinely must not change
and that a clumsy fix really would break:

- **GREEN CONTROL 4 — a platform admin still succeeds.** All three of `SYSTEM_ADMIN`,
  `SUPER_ADMIN` and `super_admin` must still pass the gate. This is the real over-correction guard:
  narrowing a role array is one typo away from locking everyone out, and this cell catches that. It
  also pins BOTH JWT casings, which the file's own comment at lines 37-39 says the seeded platform
  admin depends on. Green at the tip AND after.
- **GREEN CONTROL 5 — the surface is still there.** The write routes stay mounted (at least four:
  allocate, revoke, unrevoke, create) and an anonymous caller still gets `401`, not `403` and not
  `404`. This is what proves the fix narrowed authorisation rather than accidentally unmounting the
  router or turning the gate into a blanket refusal — a suite that passed because nothing was
  reachable any more would fail here. Green at the tip AND after.

The final `COMPLETENESS` cell is the transcription arm: it asserts `CELLS_RUN === EXPECTED_CELLS`
(5). **Proved to fire:** with the five graded cells deleted and the product fix applied — the state
a truncated or placeholder file would be in — the suite reports
`AssertionError: expected +0 to be 5` and `Tests 1 failed (1)`. A file that summarises instead of
transcribing cannot pass.

### READ THIS BEFORE YOU WRITE THE FILE

- The file you write is **exactly 105 lines**. Count them before you finish. If your output has
  fewer than 100 lines you have dropped something and must start again.
- Write **every line of the fenced block above, verbatim**, from the opening `/**` to the final
  `});`. It is not long. It fits.
- Do **NOT** summarise, abbreviate, elide, or replace any part of it with a comment.
- Do **NOT** write "due to length constraints, only the first line is shown here", "... rest of the
  file unchanged", "// remaining tests omitted", "// (similar to above)", `...`, or any other
  placeholder. Those exact phrases are the specific failure this block exists to prevent: a
  one-line file still runs, asserts nothing, exits 0, and passes every other gate.
- All six `it(` blocks must be present, in order: `RED 1`, `RED 2`, `RED 3`, `GREEN CONTROL 4`,
  `GREEN CONTROL 5`, `COMPLETENESS`. Five of them contain the line `CELLS_RUN += 1;`.
- There is no backslash character anywhere in the file.

## Red cells

(Read by `build_input.sh` → `defect_line.red_cells`; A4 counts a cell named here as a declared
red when its failure is an ASSERTION. These three titles carry no 🔴 glyph, so without this
section A4 classifies every one of them as a CONTROL red and the run FAILS on a correct output.
Added 2026-09-16 after exactly that happened — see `IMPROVEMENTS.md`.)

- `RED 1: ISSUER_ADMIN is not on the status write gate`
- `RED 2: every role on the write gate is a platform role`
- `RED 3: every tenant-scoped admin is refused 403 on POST revoke`

## Notes for the raise (not for the model)

- **Pre-measured before this brief was written**, in a `git clone --shared` scratch clone checked
  out at `48e65c435`, with `node_modules` symlinked from the source tree, every command run from a
  working directory OUTSIDE the clone (`npm --prefix <clone>/…/vc-issuer test`) so a PWD-relative
  resolve could not pass by accident. New suite alone: `3 failed | 3 passed (6)` at the tip →
  `6 passed (6)` with the two edits. Whole `vc-issuer` suite: `3 failed | 111 passed (114)` at the
  tip → `113 passed (113)` after. `npm run build` (`tsc`) clean after.
- **The sibling suite's test COUNT drops, 14 → 13, and that is correct.**
  `ks586-status-write-authorization.test.ts:96` is `it.each(STATUS_WRITE_ROLES.map(...))`, so it
  generates one case per role in the array; removing a role removes a case. Zero failures either
  way. **Say this in the PR description** — a reviewer who sees a test count fall on a security PR
  and is not told why will reasonably assume coverage was deleted.
- **Blast radius is two files, measured.** `STATUS_WRITE_ROLES` appears at exactly four lines in the
  repo: its declaration and its use in `status.ts`, and the import plus the `it.each` in the KS-586
  suite. No other service, frontend or connector reads it. (Positive control on the same corpus:
  `requireRole` appears in 39 files, so the scan can match real consumers.)
- **This narrowing is INTERIM and the brief says so in the code.** Kam's ruling was explicitly
  "narrow now, **bind-creator later**", and the ownership model is already decided —
  `secuura-ks1116-presentation-credential-ownership-model`, choice `bind-creator` (2026-09-13):
  owner is the authenticated principal that created the resource, and existing NULL-owner rows are
  readable by the tenant `OWNER` role only. **Raise the follow-up ticket with this PR:** give
  `credential_status_lists` an owner column, persist the Map to it, populate the owner at
  `POST /api/status` from the creating principal, then restore an admin-class gate plus a per-list
  `decideTenantAccess` comparison — at which point `ISSUER_ADMIN` returns. Without that ticket this
  narrowing becomes the accidental permanent answer, which is precisely how KS-586's deferred half
  went untracked for two weeks and had to be re-filed as KS-692.
- **The operational cost, stated rather than discovered later.** After this merges, no tenant-scoped
  admin can create a status list, allocate an index, revoke or un-revoke — only platform admins can.
  I measured the blast radius before recommending it: there are **zero product callers** of the
  `/api/status` write surface anywhere outside the route file, its own test, the api-gateway proxy
  mount (`proxy.ts:871`) and deployment config. Positive controls on the same corpus: `/api/keys`
  appears in 32 files, `/api/credentials` in 17. So nothing that exists today breaks — but the
  moment a frontend wires revocation up, it will need a platform token until the follow-up lands.
- **The stale-pointer fix is folded in deliberately.** KS-692's own comment (2026-09-01) records
  that `status.ts:36`'s "tracked on KS-586" caused a real round trip on Peter's PR #730 review, and
  judged it "not worth its own PR; worth not losing". EDIT 1 is that fix, taken while the file is
  open — and it is load-bearing here for a second reason: with `ISSUER_ADMIN` silently absent from
  an admin-class list, a comment explaining why is the only thing stopping someone restoring it.
- **What did not match the file at `48e65c435`:** nothing in the ticket's description of the defect
  — the root cause, the line references and the Map are all exactly as filed. Two pieces of its
  surrounding context have gone stale: it describes **KS-643 as a live parallel hold awaiting Kam**,
  but KS-643 is `Done` and archived (2026-09-13, PR #744), and its ruled helper
  `decideTenantAccess` is now the thing the follow-up should reuse; and it calls **KS-539 "open"**,
  but KS-539 is `Deployed to UAT` and archived (2026-09-14). KS-621, the tenancy-boundary ticket the
  ticket says the hold hangs on, is still `Backlog` and unruled — this narrowing does not resolve
  it and should not be recorded as doing so.
- **KS-588 stays blocked** regardless of this PR: it is the Schemathesis coverage for exactly this
  surface and lives under `systemTest/`, which is out of scope at this tip.

